#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Read-only Deploy Readiness evaluator. (CC V1-06 / #101, revised by #101.1)

What it answers
---------------
"Can we deploy now, and if not, why not" -- from evidence that already exists,
without creating anything.

    DEPLOY_READY = YES       every mandatory gate PASSes
    DEPLOY_READY = NO        at least one mandatory gate FAILs
    DEPLOY_READY = UNKNOWN   no mandatory gate FAILed, but at least one could
                             not be established

UNKNOWN is the honest answer for a mandatory live fact nobody could prove, and
it is never rounded up to YES. YES is an observation about the evidence: it is
not an approval, and it authorises nothing.

Why this revision exists
------------------------
The first revision treated CANARY and RELEASE_GATES as advisory and reported the
plan's canary declaration without re-deriving it. That allowed a verdict of YES
while the live Boss Request Bridge, which is the only component that actually
decides whether a DEPLOY Request is accepted, would deterministically refuse the
same plan. A readiness verdict that can disagree with the live gate in that
direction is worse than no verdict: it reads as permission.

So the live Bridge's own bundle contract is now the fact source. Its rules are
ported here verbatim in behaviour (see BRIDGE_RULES / bridge_verdict), and the
`BRIDGE_ACCEPTANCE` gate fails whenever those rules would refuse. Every gate is
mandatory; there are no advisory gates left.

The RELEASE_GATES gate is gone, and the four product-release declarations the plan
used to carry with it. It asked readiness to report PASS for three_end_ux,
six_vertical_closed_loop, sealed_node and final_release -- product acceptance verdicts
that belong to the upstream side, that no machine process in this repository can
produce, and that the live executor never reads. Keeping them here would have made this
evaluator report on product desirability instead of deployability, which is the opposite
of the boundary Issue #103 draws. The one name among them that stood for a real
technical fact, sealed_node, is established instead by the signed TEST_PR the plan now
carries, and the ported TEST_PR proof checks it.

What it reads (read-only, and never anything else)
--------------------------------------------------
    --control-state   the derived CURRENT_CONTROL_STATE.json from the projector
    --go-repo         a GO checkout, for the two canonical pointer files the
                      state document names AND for the published verifier
                      identities (identity/VERIFIER_IDENTITIES_V1.json and its
                      keys). Two signing roles are published, the Task signer and
                      the HK Agent Evidence signer; if the published contract
                      expresses one key in both roles this evaluator refuses to
                      claim a proven Task or a proven proof.
    --live-bundle     OPTIONAL operator-supplied directory of live-host facts.
                      The registered plan and the deployed Command Center
                      configuration are live facts an offline evaluator cannot
                      read, so when the bundle is absent those gates are UNKNOWN
                      by construction rather than assumed. Files it may contain:
                        <plan_id>.json        the registered plan bundle
                        channel.json          the deployed channel configuration

The Human Approval authority
----------------------------
An approval is not signed, and no approval public key exists. The 2026-09-16
scope reset made the authority an *authenticated GitHub identity* identified by
the two logins this project authorises, so the approval is judged on the
identity it names and on its binding to the plan, the scope and the environment.
GitHub itself decides who authored the Request PR; the live Bridge refuses an
unauthorised author before it reads the plan. This evaluator fails closed on the
same identity set, but it is a read-only evaluator and cannot re-perform the
platform's authentication, so on this one gate the live Bridge remains the
enforcement point and this verdict is the pre-check, not the authority.

What it can never do
--------------------
It holds no private key, signs nothing, creates or publishes no Task, grants no
deployment authorisation, accepts no caller-supplied image/service/path/environment/
command, contacts no host, and does not touch Production. Every one of those is
a `false` in the `authority_boundary` block of its own output.
"""
